    { q: 'Why are metals ductile and ceramics brittle?', choices: ['metallic bonds are stronger', 'dislocations can move in metals, absorbing energy by plastic flow; in ceramics they are locked in place', 'ceramics are always colder', 'metals are always polycrystalline'], answer: 1, why: 'Ductility is mobile dislocations: they let metal yield, blunt crack tips and dissipate energy before fracture. Ceramic bonding gives huge lattice friction — dislocations cannot glide, so a crack tip has no way to relieve stress but to advance.' },
    { q: 'Steel with K_IC = 50 MPa√m loaded at 300 MPa tolerates a through crack of roughly…', choices: ['5 micrometres', '9 millimetres', '9 centimetres', 'any size — steel never cracks'], answer: 1, why: 'From K = Yσ√(πa): a = (K/Yσ)²/π ≈ (50/300)²/π ≈ 8.8 mm. Design against flaws = compute the critical crack size, then inspect for cracks well below it.' },
    { q: 'Fatigue failures are dangerous because they occur…', choices: ['at stresses above yield, which engineers ignore', 'below the yield strength, after many normal-looking load cycles, often with no visible warning', 'only in aircraft', 'instantly on first loading'], answer: 1, why: 'Cyclic slip accumulates damage at stress concentrators; a crack grows a little each cycle (Paris law) until it reaches critical size — on a component that has "worked fine for years".' },
