---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/materials-science/advanced/failure-fracture-and-fatigue">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an advanced Materials Science lesson. It assumes the foundational lesson <em>Why
    Materials Behave</em> and the intermediate lessons <em>Atoms to Crystals</em>, which built
    crystal structure and began the defect census, and <em>Metals, Ceramics, Polymers,
    Composites</em>, which separated the material families whose failure modes this page now
    dissects. The advanced siblings <em>Phase Diagrams</em> and <em>Heat Treatment</em> supply
    helpful microstructure vocabulary, but nothing here depends on them.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>classify point and line defects, and explain why dislocation motion lets metals yield a thousand times below the perfect-lattice stress;</li>
    <li>contrast ductile and brittle failure, and say why the design question is "how much warning?" and not only "how strong?";</li>
    <li>use Griffith's stress-concentration and energy arguments to explain why a brittle solid's strength is set by its largest flaw;</li>
    <li>compute a critical crack size from fracture toughness, and connect the toughness league table to material selection;</li>
    <li>read the fatigue toolkit — S–N curves, Miner's rule, Paris-law growth — and explain how damage-tolerant design sets inspection intervals.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Two chapters of materials science meet in this lesson. The first is <strong>defects</strong>:
    no crystal is perfect, and the imperfections — vacancies, misplaced atoms, dislocation
    lines — are not blemishes but the working parts of materials. Plasticity <em>is</em>
    dislocation motion; strength is the art of obstructing it. The second is
    <strong>failure</strong>: how structures break, and how engineers design so they do not.
    They are one subject, because failure is defect physics at the component scale: ductility
    is defects doing their job, brittle fracture is a crack (a defect) outrunning them, and
    fatigue is defects accumulating in slow motion. A reader who comes out of this lesson can
    look at any broken part — or any intact one — and see the defect population that will
    decide its fate.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="failure modes, Griffith, toughness, fatigue" widget="equationStep" config={fractureCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the four cards are one argument in sequence. Card 1
    frames everything that follows as a safety question — warning versus silence. Card 2
    shows how a flaw multiplies stress, card 3 converts that insight into a tabulated
    material property you can design against, and card 4 runs the same flaw physics one
    cycle at a time. Watch each card reuse the previous one's vocabulary: stress
    concentration feeds the toughness number, and the toughness number feeds the
    crack-growth arithmetic that keeps old aircraft flying.
  </p>

  <h2>Defects in solids: point and line</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Point defects</strong> are zero-dimensional: the <strong>vacancy</strong> (a
    missing atom — every crystal has them, in thermal equilibrium), the
    <strong>self-interstitial</strong> (an extra atom jammed between lattice sites — rare,
    hugely strained), the <strong>substitutional</strong> impurity (a foreign atom on a
    lattice site — zinc in copper) and the <strong>interstitial</strong> solute (a small
    atom in a gap — carbon in iron). Vacancies are equilibrium features, not accidents:
    their concentration is exp(−Qᵥ/kT), so for copper near melting roughly one lattice site
    in 10⁵ is empty — millions of vacancies per grain, diffusing constantly. All solid-state
    diffusion (the heat-treatment lesson) is vacancy traffic; all solid-solution
    strengthening is solute atoms obstructing dislocations.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="file each point defect by its role" widget="sort-buckets" config={msPointBuckets} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> a defect-free crystal would be the ideal material.
    Take the defects out of a metal and you get a different problem: with no
    dislocations able to move, it cannot yield — it stays elastic until it snaps in
    brittle fashion at enormous stress. Defects give metals their forgiving failure
    mode; strength engineering is not the elimination of defects but the management of
    which ones move, where, and when.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="find the line the mechanism refutes" widget="spot-error" config={msPerfectSpot} />
  <p>
    <strong>Line defects</strong> — <strong>dislocations</strong> — are the reason metals
    bend. An <strong>edge dislocation</strong> is a terminated half-plane of atoms, a
    "extra row" squeezed into the lattice; a <strong>screw dislocation</strong> is a
    helical distortion. The disturbance is characterised by the <strong>Burgers vector</strong>,
    the closure error of a loop drawn around the line. Here is the mechanism: instead of
    shearing an entire plane of atoms at once (a stress of order the shear modulus, ~30 GPa
    for iron), the crystal moves an edge dislocation forward one lattice row at a time —
    like shuffling a carpet across a floor by pushing a ruck along it. The real stress to
    move a dislocation is typically a thousand times less than the perfect-lattice
    calculation, which is why metals yield at tens of megapascals instead of gigapascals.
    The corollary is the whole theory of strength: <strong>anything that obstructs
    dislocation motion strengthens the metal</strong> — other dislocations (work hardening:
    cold-rolled steel), solute atoms (solid-solution strengthening), grain boundaries (the
    Hall–Petch relation σ = σ₀ + k·d^(−½): halve the grain size and strength rises with
    the inverse square root), precipitates (the heat-treatment lesson's ageing), and
    interstitial carbon in martensite. Metallurgy's strength menu is a list of dislocation
    fences.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="pair each strengthening recipe with its dislocation fence" widget="match" config={msFenceMatch} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> defects are the working parts of a solid — vacancies carry
    diffusion, dislocations carry plasticity, and strength engineering is the management of
    which defects move, where, and when. Next we ask what happens when the moving defects
    stop doing their job: at a crack tip, a material without mobile dislocations has no way
    to blunt the danger, and the difference shows up as warning versus silence.
  </p>

  <h2>Ductile versus brittle: the warning question</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Ductile failure</strong> announces itself: the part yields, necks, and absorbs
    energy by plastic flow before separating — dislocations doing their job right up to the
    end. <strong>Brittle failure</strong> gives no warning: crack propagation at, or below,
    full design load, at acoustic speeds, with almost no energy absorbed. The difference is
    structural, not mystical: a ductile metal's plastic zone at a crack tip <em>blunts</em>
    the tip and consumes energy; a brittle ceramic's immobile dislocations leave the tip
    sharp, so nothing but bond-breaking resists the crack. The engineering translation is
    fail-soft versus fail-sudden — which is why pressure vessels, bridges and aircraft are
    specified ductile, and why the design question is never only "how strong?" but "how
    much warning?".
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="walk the warning question" widget="branch" config={msWarningBranch} />

  <h2>Griffith: the flaw sets the strength</h2>
  <p>
    Why is glass 100 times weaker than its bonds demand? A. A. Griffith's answer (1920)
    begins with <strong>stress concentration</strong>: at the tip of an elliptical flaw,
    local stress is amplified to σ_local ≈ 2σ·√(a/ρ), where a is the flaw's half-length and
    ρ its tip radius. Numbers: a 1-micron scratch with an atomically sharp tip (ρ ≈
    0.2 nm) under a nominal 100 MPa concentrates to 2 × 100 × √(10⁻⁶/2×10⁻¹⁰) ≈
    14,000 MPa — well above the ~7 GPa needed to break silicate bonds. Every flaw is a
    magnifying glass. Griffith's deeper step was an energy balance: a crack grows when the
    elastic energy released exceeds the surface energy cost of new faces, giving the brittle
    strength σ_c = √(2Eγ/πa). For glass (E = 70 GPa, γ ≈ 0.3 J/m², a = 1 µm) this predicts
    σ_c ≈ 116 MPa — versus a theoretical defect-free strength near 7 GPa: the
    <strong>strength of a brittle solid is set by its largest flaw</strong>, which is also
    why statistics (not a single number) governs ceramic strength, why glass gets stronger
    when its surface is fire-polished or chemically etched, and why fibre composites can be
    absurdly strong — draw the material thin enough and the largest possible flaw shrinks
    below a micron.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="call the stress at the tip" widget="estimate" config={msGriffithEstimate} />

  <h2>Fracture toughness: designing with the flaw you know you have</h2>
  <p>
    Griffith assumed atomically sharp cracks and brittle behaviour; modern
    <strong>fracture mechanics</strong> generalises it to engineering materials. A crack of
    length a under stress σ experiences the <strong>stress intensity</strong>
    K = Y·σ·√(πa) (Y a geometry factor near 1). The crack advances when K reaches
    <strong>K_IC</strong>, the plane-strain fracture toughness — a genuine material property,
    tabulated like yield strength. The design logic inverts nicely: for steel with
    K_IC = 50 MPa√m at working stress 300 MPa, the critical crack size is
    a = (K/Yσ)²/π ≈ 8.8 mm — so inspection must find cracks far below 8.8 mm (a safety
    factor on <em>length</em>), and the material choice is a race between stress, crack
    growth and detectability. The K_IC league table (MPa√m): steels roughly 50–200,
    titanium 40–100, aluminium alloys 20–40, polymers 1–5, glass and ceramics below 2 —
    the quantitative reason aircraft skins are aluminium (and composites) and windows are
    not structural. High-strength steels present the classic trap: raising yield strength
    <em>shrinks</em> the critical crack size faster than it raises it, so "stronger" can
    mean less flaw-tolerant — the Liberty ships' welded hulls, cracking in cold North
    Atlantic water at stresses they had carried fine, taught the discipline this lesson
    permanently (their other teacher was the ductile–brittle transition temperature:
    BCC steels turn brittle below a transition point; FCC metals like aluminium and copper
    do not).
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="the toughness league, upper bounds plotted" widget="chart" config={msToughnessChart} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> stress concentration turned flaws from blemishes into the
    decisive feature of strength, and fracture toughness turned that insight into a number —
    a critical crack size you can inspect below. But the Comet and Aloha failures came at
    stresses their hulls had carried for years. Next we follow a crack that grows a little
    every cycle.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> the strongest material is the safest. Strength and
    toughness are different axes: raising the strength raises the stress a part can
    carry but <em>shrinks</em> the critical crack size that would break it, so the
    stronger part can be the one that fails from a smaller, harder-to-find flaw.
    Safety lives in the pair (toughness, inspectability) plus ductility's warning —
    not in the strength number alone.
  </p>

  <h2>Fatigue: death by repetition</h2>
  <p>
    A component cycled below its yield strength — an aircraft fuselage pressurised and
    depressurised, a crankshaft turning — can still fail after thousands or millions of
    cycles. <strong>Fatigue</strong> accounts for the large majority of mechanical service
    failures. The mechanism is microscopic slip: even nominally elastic loading produces
    local plasticity at stress concentrators (fillets, rivet holes, inclusions — always
    defects again), and reversed slip extrudes and intrudes microscopic
    <strong>persistent slip bands</strong> that become crack starters. The crack then grows
    a little every cycle — the <strong>Paris law</strong> da/dN = C(ΔK)^m says growth rate
    scales with the stress-intensity range, with m typically 2–4, so a few mm of growth per
    million cycles — until the critical size from fracture mechanics is reached and the part
    snaps, its final fracture surface showing the signature <strong>beach marks</strong>
    (rings from each duty period) around a sudden fibrous snap zone. Design tools: the
    <strong>S–N curve</strong> (stress amplitude versus cycles to failure; steels show an
    endurance limit near half tensile strength, below which life is effectively infinite —
    aluminium shows none, so aircraft parts are life-limited by mandatory retirement);
    <strong>Miner's rule</strong> Σ nᵢ/Nᵢ = 1 accumulating damage from mixed load
    spectra; and above all <strong>damage-tolerant design</strong> — assume the crack is
    already there, compute its growth per flight, and set inspection intervals so it is
    reliably found before critical size. The Comet (1954) and Aloha 243 (1988) —
    pressurisation cycles fatiguing fuselages from window corners and from hidden lap-joint
    cracking — are the founding and reinforcing case files.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="rebuild the fatigue life, stage by stage" widget="order" config={msFatigueOrder} />
  <p>
    A worked example shows what damage-tolerant design means quantitatively. Inspection
    finds a 2 mm crack at a fastener hole in aluminium skin; the Paris-law calculation
    for this aircraft's stress spectrum gives a conservative average growth of
    0.7 mm per 10,000 flights, and the fracture-toughness arithmetic puts the critical
    size at 9 mm. Remaining life is about 7 ÷ 0.00007 = 100,000 flights, so the
    inspection interval is set well under half of that — say 30,000 flights — because
    the crack accelerates as it lengthens and the detection method's reliability, not
    the metal's, is the weakest link in the chain. The number produced is not a
    lifetime but a schedule: crack growth is predictable enough to be managed.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> a part that has carried the load for years is proven
    safe for more years. Fatigue damage accumulates monotonically — every cycle spends
    a share of life (Miner's rule), whether or not anything looks different — so the
    unbroken past is evidence about cracks <em>not yet grown</em>, not immunity from
    them. The honest inference from "worked fine for a decade" is that inspection
    intervals, not intuition, say what comes next.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="fill the honest inference" widget="cloze" config={msProvenCloze} />

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Recall: name the two defect families this lesson organises, and give one example of each doing useful work in a metal.</li>
    <li>Application: inspection finds a 2 mm crack at a fastener hole in aluminium skin; growth averages 0.7 mm per 10,000 flights and the critical size is 9 mm. Roughly how many flights of margin remain, and why is the inspection interval set well under it?</li>
    <li>Why can raising a steel's yield strength make it <em>less</em> safe against cracking, even though the part carries more stress?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Point defects (vacancies, solutes) and line defects
    (dislocations). Vacancies carry solid-state diffusion; dislocation motion is plasticity
    itself — which is why a defect-free crystal would fail without yielding. (2) Margin is
    7 mm of growth, about 100,000 flights; the interval sits well under half of that because
    crack growth accelerates as the crack lengthens and the detection method's reliability —
    not the metal's — is the weakest link. (3) Strength raises the stress the part can carry
    but shrinks the critical crack size that would break it, so the stronger part fails from
    a smaller, harder-to-find flaw. Safety lives in toughness plus inspectability, plus
    ductility's warning — not in the strength number alone.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Failure is defect physics at the component scale: ductility is defects doing their job, brittle fracture is a crack outrunning them, fatigue is defects accumulating in slow motion.</li>
    <li>Dislocation motion lets metals yield a thousand times below the perfect-lattice stress; every strengthening recipe is a fence for dislocations.</li>
    <li>Ductile versus brittle is the warning question: a plastic zone blunts crack tips and buys warning; immobile dislocations leave the tip sharp.</li>
    <li>Griffith: stress concentrates at flaw tips, so the largest flaw — not the bond strength — sets a brittle solid's strength.</li>
    <li>Fracture toughness K_IC turns flaws into a design calculation: a critical crack size that inspection must reliably beat.</li>
    <li>Fatigue kills below yield strength, one cycle at a time; the S–N curve, Miner's rule and Paris-law growth convert accumulation into a schedule.</li>
    <li>Damage-tolerant design assumes the crack is already there and sets inspection intervals so it is found before critical size.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The advanced sibling <em>Heat Treatment</em> shows how the dislocation-fence menu is
    built by itinerary, and how an untempered or quench-cracked microstructure hands this
    lesson its failures. <em>Phase Diagrams</em> supplies the equilibrium map those
    treatments navigate, and <em>Semiconductors and Doping</em> shows defect engineering
    aimed at conduction instead of strength.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Environment joins the conspiracy.</strong> Stress plus chemistry breaks things
    that neither alone would: <strong>stress-corrosion cracking</strong> (a static load in a
    specific medium — brass in ammonia, steel in chlorides), <strong>hydrogen
    embrittlement</strong> (atomic hydrogen from welding or corrosion entering high-strength
    steel and destroying its toughness — the reason ultra-high-strength bolts carry special
    plating rules), and <strong>cavitation-creep</strong> at high temperature (grain-boundary
    voids growing under sustained load — jet-engine discs are life-limited by creep-fatigue
    interaction). Time, temperature, chemistry and load form a four-dimensional failure
    space; every real component lives somewhere in it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Detection is part of the strength.</strong> Since designs now assume flaws exist,
    <strong>non-destructive testing</strong> is half the safety system: dye penetrant for
    surface cracks, ultrasonic pulse-echo for internal ones (sizing cracks to feed Paris-law
    growth calculations), eddy currents for surface-breaking fatigue in aircraft skin,
    radiography for welds, and acoustic emission for cracks announcing themselves. The
    quantitative discipline is "probability of detection" curves — an inspection interval is
    only valid if the method reliably finds cracks an order below critical size. Fracture
    mechanics thus quietly converted metallurgy into an information problem: the material's
    toughness sets the critical flaw, and the maintenance programme's sensors must find
    flaws smaller than it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Fatigue remains partly empirical: life prediction from
    microstructure alone (crystal-plasticity models of persistent slip bands) is improving
    but not yet predictive enough to replace testing, and <em>very high cycle</em> fatigue
    (beyond 10⁹ cycles — relevant to wind turbines and bearings) revealed a surprise: S–N
    curves that looked flat develop a new knee and failures at "infinite" lives. Small-crack
    behaviour violates classic Paris scaling (cracks shorter than the microstructure grow
    faster than the law says), which matters because initiation consumes most of a
    component's life. And for additively manufactured parts, the defect population itself is
    process-dependent (pores from trapped gas, lack-of-fusion), making the coupling between
    printing parameters and fatigue life one of the field's most active frontiers.
  </p>

